.TH DANBY-AEROBRAKING 1
.SH NAME
danby-aerobraking \- orbital energy loss during atmospheric passes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B danby/aerobraking
.SH MODEL
Central gravity produces the orbit while exponential atmosphere applies drag
near planetary radius. Repeated periapsis passages lower speed and apoapsis.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Danby-aerobraking
is one file, nothing else: no plugin, no library, no harness. The
problem (central gravity plus atmospheric drag) is written as
.IR eqn (7)
expressions; the diagnostic plot is written in
.IR plot (2)'s
own graph command language; the interface (parameter sliders,
pause/reset) is real Tk, built directly from the same
.B param
lines the physics uses. All of it, problem and interface alike, lives
in one script string this command runs at load time \(em there is no
separate module interface, no separate parsing library, and no
separate harness loaded by path.
.PP
The script is not parsed into a structure and then acted on; it is
.I executed
one line at a time, like a shell script.
.B Cmd
is a command dispatcher, the same shape as
.IR tk (2)'s
own
.B tk->cmd()
and
.IR plot (2)'s
own
.BR Plotter.cmd() ,
and each line takes effect immediately as it runs \(em compiling its
own
.IR eqn (7)
expression right there, growing the running parameter/state/equation
tables right there \(em rather than being staged into a list for a
separate pass afterward. A
.B derivative
line's matching
.B state
line must therefore already have run, the same "define before use"
order any ordinary script already requires.
.SS "The script's own command language"
One command per line:
.TP
.BI "title " TEXT
Rest of the line, verbatim.
.TP
.BI "bodylabels " "WORD ..."
.TP
.BI "mass " "NUM ..."
One value per
.B bodylabels
word, same order.
.TP
.BI "param " LABEL " " DEFAULT " " MIN " " MAX
Repeatable; also becomes a real Tk scale widget, live-adjustable while
the model runs.
.TP
.BI "maxtime " NUM
.TP
.BI "state " NAME " " EXPR ...
Repeatable; declares one named state variable. Its position among all
.B state
commands run so far is its index in the state/derivative arrays.
.I EXPR
is its initial value, an
.IR eqn (7)
expression that may reference any
.BR param 's
own name.
.TP
.BI "derivative " NAME " " EXPR ...
Repeatable, one per
.B state
command, matched by
.IR NAME .
.I EXPR
may reference any state's own name, any param, or any
.BR eq .
.TP
.BI "eq " NAME " " EXPR ...
Repeatable; a named intermediate quantity, evaluated in declaration
order before every
.BR derivative ,
so a later
.B eq
or any
.B derivative
may reference an earlier
.B eq
by name.
.TP
.BI "observable " LABEL " " EXPR ...
Repeatable; same binding as
.BR derivative / eq .
Exactly two required \(em the diagnostic plot always shows two.
.TP
.BI "graph " TEXT
Repeatable, rest of the line verbatim, passed straight to
.IR plot (2)'s
own
.BR Plotter.cmd() :
.BR table / colour
run once, at setup;
.BR axis / line
are reissued every redraw, since
.IR plot (2)'s
own
.B clear
command wipes view/layer state.
.B table
must NOT be reissued \(em
.IR plot (2)'s
own
.B bind()
unconditionally replaces a table with a fresh, empty one, so
reissuing it every redraw would silently erase every appended data
point right after it was recorded.
.PP
Every
.I EXPR
is
.IR eqn (7)
syntax, compiled once, when its command runs, via
.IR hoc (2)'s
own
.BR compileeqn() ,
which does the eqn(7)\(->hoc(2) translation internally \(em this file
never loads or names
.IR eqn2hoc (2)
itself. Every simulation step only rebinds symbols and evaluates the
already-compiled trees.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/danby/aerobraking.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR eqn2hoc (2),
.IR hoc (2),
.IR plot (2),
.IR tk (2),
.IR eqnorbitaltest (1)
.SH BUGS
The "planet" body's four state components never move \(em a real
property of this specific model, not a limitation of the command
language, which allows any number of
.B state
commands but has nothing yet exercising real N-body coupling through
it.
